      "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22514350",
      "title": "Identification of the galactose binding domain of the adeno-associated virus serotype 9 capsid.",
      "publish_date": "2012-04-18",
      "excerpts": [
        "In addition, AAV9 is unique compared to other AAV serotypes in that it is capable of surpassing the blood-brain barrier and transducing neurons in the brain and spinal cord. It has recently been shown that AAV9 uses galactose as a receptor to transduce many different cell types in vitro, as well as cells of the mouse airway in vivo.\nIn this study, we sought to identify the specific amino acids of the AAV9 capsid necessary for binding to galactose.\nBy site-directed mutagenesis and cell binding assays, plus computational ligand docking studies, we discovered five amino acids, including N470, D271, N272, Y446, and W503, which are required for galactose binding that form a pocket at the base of the protrusions around the icosahedral 3-fold axes of symmetry.\nThe importance of these amino acids for tissue tropism was also confirmed by in vivo studies in the mouse lung. Identifying the interactions necessary for AAV9 binding to galactose may lead to advances in vector engineering."
      ]
    },
    {
      "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40340250",
      "title": "Highly conserved brain vascular receptor ALPL mediates transport of engineered AAV vectors across the blood-brain barrier - PubMed",
      "publish_date": null,
      "excerpts": [
        "Highly conserved brain vascular receptor ALPL mediates transport of engineered AAV vectors across the blood-brain barrier [Tyler C Moyer](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Moyer+TC&cauthor_id=40340250) ¹ , [Brett A Hoffman](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Hoffman+BA&cauthor_id=40340250) ¹ , [Weitong ... Authors [Tyler C Moyer](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Moyer+TC&cauthor_id=40340250) ¹ , [Brett A Hoffman](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Hoffman+BA&cauthor_id=40340250) ¹ , [Weitong Chen](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Chen+W&cauthor_id=40340250) ¹ , [Ishan ... Abstract Bioengineered adeno-associated virus (AAV) capsids have been shown to penetrate the BBB with great efficacy in mouse and non-human primate models, but their translational potential is often limited by species selectivity and undefined mechanisms of action. Here, we apply our RNA-guided TRACER AAV capsid evolution platform to generate VCAP-102, an AAV9 variant with markedly increased brain tropism following intravenous delivery in both rodents and primates. Relative to AAV9, VCAP-102 demonstrates 20- to 400-fold increased gene transfer across multiple brain regions. We identify alkaline phosphatase (ALPL) as the primary receptor used by VCAP-102 to cross the BBB and demonstrate that direct binding of VCAP-102 to human ALPL can initiate receptor-mediated transcytosis in a cell barrier model. Our work identifies VCAP-102 as a cross-species CNS gene delivery vector with a strong potential for clinical translation and establishes ALPL as a brain delivery shuttle capable of efficient BBB transport to maximize CNS delivery of biotherapeutics. **Keywords:** AAV; ALPL; blood-brain barrier; brain; directed evolution; non-human primate. PubMed Disclaimer ... Figures TRACER-based _in vivo_ screen design and top hits (A) Design of TRACER-NHP directed… **Figure 1** TRACER-based _in vivo_ screen design and top hits (A) Design of TRACER-NHP directed evolution pipeline. (B) Heatmap of top 100 most enriched capsids in macaque brain from step (8). Color scale represents the mRNA enrichment score of the top 100 capsids vs. AAV9 in indicated tissues. SC: spinal cord. (C) Comparative performance of 1,500 capsids in cynomolgus macaque and C57Bl/6J mouse brain. Capsids with >10-fold enrichment relative to AAV9 in macaque brain are highlighted in orange. (D) VR-IV peptide inserts from top capsids identified in (C). ... VCAP-101 and VCAP-102 display increased CNS tropism in the mouse brain (A) Transduction of CNS and liver in BALB/c mice. Capsids with a self-complementary CAG-ZsGreen-HA transgene were dosed i.v. at 1E13 VG/kg. Tissues were stained with an anti-HA antibody 28 days after injection. ... Improved CNS tropism of VCAP-102 in African green monkey (A) Representative images of primate CNS transduction."
      ]
