    {
      "url": "https://journals.asm.org/doi/abs/10.1128/jvi.00843-21",
      "title": "Adeno-associated Virus 9 Structural Rearrangements Induced by Endosomal Trafficking pH and Glycan Attachment | Journal of Virology",
      "publish_date": "2021-07-14",
      "excerpts": [
        "In addition, the structural study of AAV9-galactose interactions demonstrates that AAV9 remains attached to its glycan receptor at the late endosome pH 5.5. This interaction significantly alters the conformational stability of the variable region I of the VPs, as well as the dynamics associated with VP N terminus externalization.\nThis is partly because AAV9 is capable of crossing the blood-brain barrier and readily transduces a wide array of tissues, including the central nervous system. In this study, we provide AAV9 capsid structural insight during intracellular trafficking.\nFurthermore, AAV9 can cross the blood-brain barrier and targets the central nervous system with greater efficiency than other AAV serotypes ( 24 – 27 ).\nGiven the resolution, the density was adequately ordered to model and reconstruct side chain orientations of the VP structure ( Fig. 1 ).\nHowever, two regions were disordered, VRI and the apex of the BC loop. Previously, VRI was reported to be associated with the blood-brain barrier (BBB) crossing ability of both AAV9 and AAVrh10 ( 26 , 68 ).\nCharacterization of the galactose-bound AAV9 capsid reveals receptor binding-induced structural changes.\nTo determine how binding to the terminal glycan of its receptor would affect the AAV9 capsid structure, purified AAV9 VLPs were complexed with terminal galactose in the form of N -acetylgalactosamine (Gal-NAc)."
      ]
